---
import Image from '@the-civia-project/assets/og/social-paper.webp';
import Article from '../components/article/Article.astro';
import ArticleAside from '../components/article/ArticleAside.astro';
import ArticleDetails from '../components/article/ArticleDetails.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../components/article/ArticleHeader.astro';
import ArticleListItem from '../components/article/ArticleListItem.astro';
import ArticlePart from '../components/article/ArticlePart.astro';
import DesignPrinciple from '../components/article/DesignPrinciple.astro';
import EditorialQuestion from '../components/article/EditorialQuestion.astro';
import EditorialTodo from '../components/article/EditorialTodo.astro';
import ExternalLink from '../components/article/ExternalLink.astro';
import InternalLink from '../components/article/InternalLink.astro';
import DiagramPlaceholder from '../components/article/DiagramPlaceholder.astro';
import H3 from '../components/article/H3.astro';
import SectionLead from '../components/article/SectionLead.astro';
import References from '../components/article/References.astro';
import PrintBackPage from '../components/article/PrintBackPage.astro';
import TableOfContents from '../components/article/TableOfContents.astro';
import U from '../components/U.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const image_url = new URL(Image.src, Astro.site);

const description =
  'The Technical Paper describes the architecture of the platform: EUDI Wallet identity under eIDAS 2.0, architecturally separated public and private lanes, public-lane algorithmic feeds with on-device intent, anonymized aggregate metrics, and open-source code with config in the repository.';

const parts = [
  {
    id: 'part-i',
    part: 'Part I',
    title: 'Identity Verification Architecture',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-ii',
    part: 'Part II',
    title: 'Interaction Architecture: Public and Private Lanes',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-iii',
    part: 'Part III',
    title: 'Privacy Architecture: Local-Only by Design',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-iv',
    part: 'Part IV',
    title: 'Anonymized Aggregate Metrics',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-v',
    part: 'Part V',
    title: 'System Synthesis and Open Governance',
  },
];

const sp = 'Social Paper';
const spI =
  'Part I: The Architecture of Openness and the Lack of Privacy Primitives';
const spII =
  'Part II: Hooked on the Feed and the Commodification of Connection';
const spIII =
  'Part III: The Next Generation: Easing Children into the Digital World';
const spIV =
  'Part IV: The Automation of Outrage and Reclaiming the Human Network';
const spV = 'Part V: The Corporate Guest in a Human Space';
const spVI =
  'Part VI: Law, Not Policy — Free Speech and Platform Responsibility';
const spVII =
  'Part VII: Lack of Accountability and Ethics in the Corporate Space';
const spVIII = 'Part VIII: The Blueprint for a Private Web';
---

<Layout size="large">
  <Fragment slot="header">
    <title>Technical Paper &mdash; The Civia Project</title>

    <meta name="description" content={description} />

    <!-- Open Graph -->
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:image" content={image_url} />

    <!-- X Card -->
    <meta name="twitter:description" content={description} />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content={image_url} />
  </Fragment>

  <Article>
    <ArticleHeader
      label="Technical Paper"
      title="Engineering a Verified, Privacy-First Social Network"
      authors={['Alexandru Ifrim', 'Ștefan Alexandru Mocanu']}
      date="2026-06-22"
      draft
    />

    <ArticleAside label="Abstract" italic>
      <p>
        This paper is the technical companion to <InternalLink href="/social-paper" paper={sp} />. Where that document argues the case for improving the social web with
        privacy in mind—an exercise in reclaiming the digital town square, not a
        finished blueprint—this one describes the architecture we are building:
        trust boundaries, data flows, and standards-aligned design decisions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <InternalLink href="/social-paper#part-vii" paper={sp} section={spVII} /> documents how for-profit platforms subordinate ethical duties to
        revenue and treat fines as operating cost. This paper's response is
        structural: open-source auditability, non-profit stewardship,
        privacy-by-architecture, and governance that does not depend on
        corporate good faith.
      </p>
      <p>
        The platform will initially target the European Union market. Every
        account is tied to a verified natural person through the <ExternalLink href="https://commission.europa.eu/topics/digital-economy-and-society/european-digital-identity_en" label="European Digital Identity Wallet — European Commission" /> under the <ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1183" label="Regulation (EU) 2024/1183 (eIDAS 2.0)" /> framework.
      </p>
      <p>
        Interaction is split into two architecturally separated lanes: a <U
          >public</U
        > lane for conventional social posting, and a <U>private</U> lane where friend-only
        posts are encrypted via Signal's <ExternalLink href="https://signal.org/docs/" label="Signal Protocol Documentation" />.
      </p>
      <p>
        Users choose their feed type — reverse-chronological or algorithmic.
        When algorithmic, feed intent is computed locally on the user's device
        and used only to query items for the feed. The algorithmic feed also
        exposes user-configurable settings — as described in <InternalLink
          href="/social-paper#part-ii-algorithmic-interventions"
          paper={sp}
          section={spII}
          heading="Algorithmic Interventions"
        />. The platform does not build persistent behavioral profiles or
        long-term interest graphs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Only coarse, anonymized engagement aggregates are collected to show post
        statistics.
      </p>
      <p>
        The codebase is <U>open source</U>. Production configuration lives in
        the repository; only keys and other secrets are supplied at runtime.
      </p>
    </ArticleAside>

    <TableOfContents items={parts} />

    <ArticlePart
      id="part-i"
      part="Part I"
      title="Identity Verification Architecture"
    >
      <SectionLead slot="lead">
        Verified identity is a structural primitive: one human, one account via
        EUDI Wallet under eIDAS 2.0. Selective disclosure and zero-knowledge
        proofs minimize what is shared; pseudonym separation keeps verification
        records cryptographically apart from social activity.
      </SectionLead>

      <ArticleDetails
        headings={[
          {
            id: 'part-i-verified-identity-as-a-primitive',
            title: 'Verified Identity as a Structural Primitive',
          },
          {
            id: 'part-i-eudi-wallet-and-eidas',
            title: 'EUDI Wallet and eIDAS 2.0',
          },
          {
            id: 'part-i-selective-disclosure-and-zkp',
            title: 'Selective Disclosure and Zero-Knowledge Proofs',
          },
          {
            id: 'part-i-pseudonym-separation',
            title: 'Pseudonym Separation',
          },
        ]}
      >
      <H3 id="part-i-verified-identity-as-a-primitive"
        >Verified Identity as a Structural Primitive</H3
      >
      <p>
        The open Web has no native mechanism to distinguish a human from a bot.
        As described in <InternalLink
          href="/social-paper#part-iv-the-ease-of-creating-and-managing-bots"
          paper={sp}
          section={spIV}
          heading="The Ease of Creating and Managing Bots"
        />, this absence has enabled bot networks, social media farms, and
        AI-driven synthetic accounts to dominate the digital town square. For a
        platform whose mission is genuine human discourse, identity verification
        is not a feature bolted on at the edge — it is a structural primitive of
        the architecture.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every user account must be linked to a verified digital identity. The
        platform enforces a simple hierarchy: one human, one account. Bots,
        automated agents, and unverified entities cannot hold user accounts.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-i-eudi-wallet-and-eidas">EUDI Wallet and eIDAS 2.0</H3>
      <p>
        Identity verification on the platform relies on the <ExternalLink href="https://commission.europa.eu/topics/digital-economy-and-society/european-digital-identity_en" label="European Digital Identity Wallet — European Commission" /> (<U>EUDI Wallet</U>), compliant with <ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1183" label="Regulation (EU) 2024/1183 (eIDAS 2.0)" /> (<ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1183" label="Regulation (EU) 2024/1183 (eIDAS 2.0)" />). Users hold government-issued digital credentials locally in their wallet.
        At registration, the platform receives only what the user explicitly chooses
        to disclose — not full identity documents, and not more attributes than the
        platform requires to operate.
      </p>
      <p>
        This approach aligns identity assurance with data minimization: the
        wallet is the user's agent, and the platform is a relying party that
        requests the minimum proof necessary.
      </p>

      <EditorialQuestion>
        When a user authenticates with their EUDI Wallet, how does the platform
        determine whether they already have an account?
      </EditorialQuestion>

      <H3 id="part-i-selective-disclosure-and-zkp"
        >Selective Disclosure and Zero-Knowledge Proofs</H3
      >
      <p>
        Where technically feasible, the platform employs <U
          >selective disclosure</U
        > and <U>zero-knowledge proofs</U> so that users can prove specific claims
        without revealing underlying personal data. For example:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Proof of natural person:</U> confirm the account holder is a real human
          without transmitting a full identity document.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Proof of legal age:</U> confirm a user has attained eighteen (18) years
          of age without revealing their precise date of birth — required for age-gated
          features such as opt-in algorithmic feeds.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Proof of citizenship:</U> citizenship is disclosed from the EUDI Wallet
          at account creation and shown on the account.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Validated legal name (optional):</U> users may choose to display a legal
          or given name on their account. When they do, the platform validates it
          against their EUDI Wallet — not a self-asserted string. If the user removes
          it, the platform deletes it completely; adding it again requires fresh wallet
          validation.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Proof of professional license (required for professional-advice mode):</U
          > selectively disclose profession, issuing jurisdiction, and validity — e.g.
          physician, lawyer, regulated financial adviser — via EUDI Wallet <U
            >EAA</U
          >/<U>QEAA</U> credentials without transmitting full license documents.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For professional credentials, the platform acts as a relying party and the wallet
        as the user's agent. Verification confirms credential existence and scope — not
        the quality of any advice given. As <InternalLink
          href="/social-paper#part-vi-professional-advice-and-verified-credentials"
          paper={sp}
          section={spVI}
          heading="Professional Advice and Verified Credentials"
        /> argues, that distinction keeps credential gating within architecture and law,
        not corporate truth-policing.
      </p>
      <p>
        The platform does not store raw identity credentials or full personal
        identifiers beyond what is strictly necessary for verification purposes,
        consistent with <ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504#art_5" label="GDPR Article 5 — Principles Relating to Processing of Personal Data" />, <ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504#art_25" label="GDPR Article 25 — Data Protection by Design and by Default" />, and <ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504#art_32" label="GDPR Article 32 — Security of Processing" /> of the <ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32016R0679" label="General Data Protection Regulation (EU) 2016/679" />.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-i-pseudonym-separation">Pseudonym Separation</H3>
      <p>
        Identity verification is required at registration, but users engage on
        the platform under a chosen pseudonym or handle. Their citizenship is
        always shown. Users may optionally display a validated legal or given
        name on their account; when they do, it must be confirmed through their
        EUDI Wallet. If they remove it, the platform deletes it completely —
        restoring it requires re-validation. The link between a user's verified
        identity and their on-platform activity is protected by strong technical
        and organizational measures:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Cryptographic separation between identity verification records and
          social activity data.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Access controls ensuring the mapping between a verified identity and a
          user account is accessible only under lawful process or explicit user
          request.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          The platform does not proactively link or publish a user's real
          identity alongside their platform activity, except where required by a
          valid legal obligation.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <DiagramPlaceholder
        title="Identity verification flow: EUDI Wallet to pseudonym separation"
        description="EUDI Wallet (government credentials) → selective disclosure / ZKP → verified account → pseudonym separation."
      />
      </ArticleDetails>

      <DesignPrinciple id="principle-i" title="People First, Bots Never">
        <p>
          Verified digital identity is the foundation of a trustworthy social
          network. By tying every account to a real, accountable person through
          EUDI Wallet credentials, selective disclosure, and pseudonym
          separation, the platform structurally excludes bots and unverified
          agents — not through moderation alone, but through architecture.
        </p>
      </DesignPrinciple>
    </ArticlePart>

    <ArticlePart
      id="part-ii"
      part="Part II"
      title="Interaction Architecture: Public and Private Lanes"
    >
      <SectionLead slot="lead">
        Every post is public or private, with separated data paths. The public
        lane supports reverse-chronological or opt-in algorithmic feeds built
        from on-device intent, vector DB queries, seen-post filters, and
        server-side ranking. Private posts use Signal Protocol encryption; the
        server relays ciphertext only.
      </SectionLead>

      <ArticleDetails
        headings={[
          {
            id: 'part-ii-two-modes-one-platform',
            title: 'Two Modes, One Platform',
          },
          {
            id: 'part-ii-public-interaction',
            title: 'Public Interaction',
          },
          {
            id: 'part-ii-private-interaction',
            title: 'Private Interaction',
          },
          {
            id: 'part-ii-friend-graph-and-boundaries',
            title: 'Friend Graph and Cross-Lane Boundaries',
          },
        ]}
      >
      <H3 id="part-ii-two-modes-one-platform">Two Modes, One Platform</H3>
      <p>
        Every post on the platform is explicitly <U>public</U> or <U>private</U
        >. Users choose visibility at publish time. The two lanes share identity
        verification and account infrastructure, but their data paths are
        architecturally separated.
      </p>
      <p>
        Visibility is not always final. A <U>private</U> post may be made <U
          >public</U
        > at any time. A <U>public</U> post may be made <U>private</U> only within
        a short grace window after publication — on the order of fifteen (15) minutes
        — and only while it has received no reactions (likes, comments, or shares).
        Once a public post has engagement, or the grace window has elapsed, it remains
        public.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-ii-public-interaction">Public Interaction</H3>
      <p>
        The public lane works like conventional social media for distribution
        and discovery. Posts are stored on platform servers, indexed into a
        vector database, and discoverable via public surfaces and <U>public</U> follow
        relationships. They are also analyzed, grouped, and categorized. The follow
        graph is secret. A user may choose to follow someone publicly. As <InternalLink
          href="/social-paper#part-ii-algorithmic-interventions"
          paper={sp}
          section={spII}
          heading="Algorithmic Interventions"
        /> argues, the feed must serve users' benefit—not outrage optimized for
        capital gains. The reverse-chronological feed is the default. Adults may
        explicitly opt in to an algorithmic feed.
      </p>
      <p>
        The algorithmic feed on the <U>public lane</U> follows a strict six-step
        pipeline. Intent is <em>not</em> sent for ranking — it is sent to <U
          >query the vector database</U
        >. Ranking happens <em>after</em> seen-post filtering.
      </p>
      <ol
        class:list={[
          // layout
          'mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6',
          // color
          'text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300',
        ]}
      >
        <li>
          The user's <U>intent vector</U> is computed <strong>locally</strong> on
          the device from on-device signals.
        </li>
        <li>
          The intent vector is sent to the <strong>server</strong>, together
          with a compact probabilistic filter snapshot for seen-post
          deduplication.
        </li>
        <li>
          The server uses the intent vector to <strong
            >query the vector database</strong
          > where post vectors are stored, returning a <strong
            >list of post IDs</strong
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          The server passes that ID list through <strong
            >probabilistic data structures</strong
          > (Bloom, Cuckoo, and/or Quotient filters) to remove already-seen posts.
        </li>
        <li>
          The server runs the <strong>ranking model</strong> on the filtered ID list.
        </li>
        <li>
          The server <strong>returns the ranked feed list</strong> to the client.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <DiagramPlaceholder
        title="Public-lane algorithmic feed pipeline: query, filter, then rank"
        description="Intent on device → send intent + filter snapshot → vector DB query → seen-post filters → ranking → ranked feed."
      />

      <H3 id="part-ii-private-interaction">Private Interaction</H3>
      <p>
        When a user publishes privately, the post is encrypted on the client
        before it leaves the device. It is delivered to all friends using Signal's
        <ExternalLink href="https://signal.org/docs/" label="Signal Protocol Documentation" /> (<ExternalLink href="https://signal.org/docs/specifications/doubleratchet/" label="The Double Ratchet Algorithm — Signal Specifications" /> and <ExternalLink href="https://signal.org/docs/specifications/x3dh/" label="The X3DH Key Agreement Protocol — Signal Specifications" />). Friends decrypt on their own devices.
      </p>
      <p>
        The private lane has no algorithmic pipeline. Friends' private posts
        surface in two feeds: they are shown <strong>intertwined</strong> with public
        posts in the default <U>reverse-chronological</U> feed, and in a <strong
          >dedicated friends-only feed</strong
        > where the user sees only their friends' posts. The server relays encrypted
        payloads and holds no decryption keys; friends decrypt on their own devices.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-ii-friend-graph-and-boundaries"
        >Friend Graph and Cross-Lane Boundaries</H3
      >
      <p>
        Private delivery requires an established friend relationship. Key
        exchange and session management follow <ExternalLink href="https://signal.org/docs/" label="Signal Protocol Documentation" /> semantics. The public and private data paths never merge without user action.
      </p>

      <DiagramPlaceholder
        title="Public and private interaction lanes"
        description="Shared identity layer; public lane (publish → store/index → feeds); private lane (encrypt → relay → decrypt → device feeds)."
      />
      </ArticleDetails>

      <DesignPrinciple
        id="principle-ii"
        title="Public Discovery, Private by Protocol"
      >
        <p>
          Users choose visibility at publish time, with transitions governed by
          the rules above. The public lane may use an on-device intent vector to
          query the vector store and run the filter-then-rank pipeline when
          adults opt in to algorithmic feeds. Private posts are encrypted and
          delivered to friends via the <ExternalLink href="https://signal.org/docs/" label="Signal Protocol Documentation" />; the server relays ciphertext only. Private feeds are assembled on the
          device — reverse-chronological and friends-only — with no server-side algorithmic
          pipeline. Two modes, one platform — architecturally separated.
        </p>
      </DesignPrinciple>
    </ArticlePart>

    <ArticlePart
      id="part-iii"
      part="Part III"
      title="Privacy Architecture: Local-Only by Design"
    >
      <SectionLead slot="lead">
        Privacy is architecture, not a setting. The public-lane server processes
        feed requests ephemerally without building profiles; the private lane
        cannot read content. Intent vectors and probabilistic seen-post filters
        live on-device; minors get chronological defaults and parental gates on
        connections and algorithmic feeds.
      </SectionLead>

      <ArticleDetails
        headings={[
          { id: 'part-iii-server-roles', title: 'Server Roles by Lane' },
          {
            id: 'part-iii-intent-computation-and-transmission',
            title: 'Intent Computation and Transmission',
          },
          {
            id: 'part-iii-seen-post-filters',
            title: 'Seen-Post Filters: Probabilistic Data Structures',
          },
          {
            id: 'part-iii-local-state-and-sync',
            title: 'Local State and Cross-Device Synchronization',
          },
          { id: 'part-iii-minor-accounts', title: 'Minor Accounts' },
          {
            id: 'part-iii-what-we-do-not-collect',
            title: 'What the Platform Does Not Collect and Will Never Collect',
          },
        ]}
      >
      <H3 id="part-iii-server-roles">Server Roles by Lane</H3>
      <p>
        As described in <InternalLink
          href="/social-paper#part-i-the-new-dna-local-only-primitives"
          paper={sp}
          section={spI}
          heading="The New DNA: Local-Only Primitives"
        />, local-only primitives are implemented here as architectural constraints—not negotiable
        settings. On the <U>public lane</U>, the server stores public posts, the
        follow graph, and content vectors. Follow relationships are secret by default;
        only follows a user chooses to make public are visible to others. It executes
        vector database queries, seen-post filtering, and ranking per feed request.
        It does not build persistent user profiling from intent vectors or filter snapshots
        received during those requests.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the <U>private lane</U>, the server is structurally incapable of
        reading private content. It relays ciphertext between friends and holds no
        decryption keys. It does not assemble, rank, or serve the private feed — that
        happens on the user's device only.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iii-intent-computation-and-transmission"
        >Intent Computation and Transmission</H3
      >
      <p>
        For the <U>public lane</U> algorithmic feed, the device computes the intent
        vector locally from on-device signals — the user controls what forms the query.
        The vector is transmitted to the server <em>only</em> to query the public vector
        database for that specific feed request. It is not a direct input to the ranking
        model, and it is not retained to build a longitudinal interest graph. The intent
        vector is <strong>never</strong> used for the private feed: the private feed exists
        on the device only, not on the server.
      </p>
      <p>
        This distinction matters: per-request intent transmission applies to public
        algorithmic retrieval only. The private feed does not use server-side vector queries.
        Persistent profiling is not.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iii-seen-post-filters"
        >Seen-Post Filters: Probabilistic Data Structures</H3
      >
      <p>
        To suppress already-viewed posts from the feed, the platform uses <U
          >probabilistic data structures</U
        > — such as <U>Bloom filters</U>, <U>Cuckoo filters</U>, and/or <U
          >Quotient filters</U
        >. The exact filter or combination is not yet finalized. These structures are
        <strong>maintained on-device</strong>. A compact snapshot is sent with each public
        algorithmic feed request so the server can apply them to the post ID list in step
        four, <em>before</em> ranking.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Why probabilistic:</U> fixed-size structures trade a controlled false-positive
          rate for orders-of-magnitude less storage than exact sets. A false positive
          may occasionally hide a post the user has not seen; this is an acceptable
          trade-off for feed deduplication at scale.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Candidates include:</U> Bloom filters for simple membership checks; Cuckoo
          filters where deletion and eviction of stale entries matters; Quotient filters
          as a space-efficient alternative with configurable false-positive bounds.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Cross-device sync:</U> filters are re-computed with synchronized information
          from linked devices.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <H3 id="part-iii-local-state-and-sync"
        >Local State and Cross-Device Synchronization</H3
      >
      <p>
        User preferences — blocks, filters, themes — and interaction history
        remain on-device across both lanes. Personal state is synchronized
        across the user's own devices via E2EE relays or direct peer-to-peer
        connections. The platform's servers act as blind couriers for sync
        traffic; they never hold decryption keys or the master copy of personal
        data.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iii-minor-accounts">Minor Accounts</H3>
      <p>
        Minor protection is governed by the principles in <InternalLink
          href="/social-paper#part-iii-guardrails-for-minor-accounts"
          paper={sp}
          section={spIII}
          heading="Guardrails for Minor Accounts"
        />. Where age restrictions, parental-consent requirements, or other
        minor-protection laws apply, the platform complies fully—regardless of
        whether we agree with every policy choice behind them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Users verified as minors receive a chronological feed by default.
        Algorithmic recommendations are available only when approved by a parent
        or guardian. Age is proven via selective disclosure or zero-knowledge
        proof without revealing the user's precise date of birth.
      </p>
      <p>
        Underage accounts operate under deliberate limitations on the social
        graph—and those limitations sit in parents' hands. Public accounts
        eligible for minor interaction must appear on <U>curated allowlists</U> vetted
        against child-safety criteria. The allowlist defines what is eligible,
        not what is automatic: parents still choose what their child may select
        from those lists.
      </p>
      <p>
        New connections—follows, direct messages, and other one-to-one or group
        links—require explicit <U>validation from a verified parent or guardian</U
        > before they take effect. Even when a public account is on the allowlist,
        a parent must still approve each specific connection.
      </p>
      <p>
        Guardrails on an underage account—connection rules, time limits, and
        other restrictions parents may tighten or relax—are <U
          >added and removed by parents</U
        > within platform minimums. The platform provides safe defaults: a chronological
        feed for minors, with algorithmic recommendations gated behind explicit parental
        approval.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iii-what-we-do-not-collect"
        >What the Platform Does Not Collect and Will Never Collect</H3
      >
      <p>The platform does not — and will never — collect or retain:</p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Persistent behavioral profiles or cross-session interest graphs.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          "Privacy toggle" mining — preferences used as psychological data
          points.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Audience profiling dossiers derived from platform activity.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>Private post plaintext.</ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Long-term server-side read-history logs.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Minimal server-side data exists for platform function: public posts, the
        follow graph (secret by default; public follows opt-in), verification tokens,
        and encrypted private payloads as ciphertext — deleted from the server after
        the client receives them and backs them up.
      </p>

      <DiagramPlaceholder
        title="Device-origin intent and filters versus ephemeral server-side processing"
        description="User device (local intent, filters, settings) vs server (query → filter → rank → feed); per-request boundary only."
      />
      </ArticleDetails>

      <DesignPrinciple
        id="principle-iii"
        title="Privacy as Structure, Not Setting"
      >
        <p>
          Privacy is woven into the architecture — not offered as a toggle
          buried in settings, as <InternalLink href="/social-paper#part-vii" paper={sp} section={spVII} /> documents across commercial platforms. Intent is computed locally;
          filters are maintained on-device; the server processes feed requests
          ephemerally without building surveillance dossiers. The private lane
          is encrypted by protocol, not by policy.
        </p>
      </DesignPrinciple>
    </ArticlePart>

    <ArticlePart id="part-iv" part="Part IV" title="Anonymized Aggregate Metrics">
      <SectionLead slot="lead">
        Only coarse anonymized counters support operational health and optional
        per-post public-lane stats for authors—no behavioral dossiers,
        third-party analytics, or private-lane engagement mining. Aggregation
        thresholds and public annual disclosure keep metrics transparent without
        surveillance.
      </SectionLead>

      <ArticleDetails
        headings={[
          { id: 'part-iv-purpose', title: 'Purpose' },
          { id: 'part-iv-what-is-collected', title: 'What Is Collected' },
          {
            id: 'part-iv-public-vs-private-metrics',
            title: 'Public vs Private Lane Implications',
          },
          { id: 'part-iv-what-is-forbidden', title: 'What Is Forbidden' },
          {
            id: 'part-iv-anonymization-and-transparency',
            title: 'Anonymization and Transparency',
          },
        ]}
      >
      <H3 id="part-iv-purpose">Purpose</H3>
      <p>
        The platform collects only coarse, anonymized metrics for operational
        health: daily active users (DAU) and monthly active users (MAU) bands,
        post volume trends, error rates, and infrastructure capacity. These
        metrics exist to keep the platform running — not to profile individuals.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iv-what-is-collected">What Is Collected</H3>
      <p>Generalized counters only, for example:</p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>Public posts created today.</ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>Active sessions only.</ArticleListItem>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Only active sessions are counted. Once a session becomes inactive, it is
        deleted after a period of inactivity; inactive sessions are purged
        periodically.
      </p>
      <p>
        No per-user behavioral dossiers are constructed from these aggregates.
      </p>

      <EditorialTodo title="Think about metrics">
        <p class="m-0 font-medium">Platform feature usage (anonymised)</p>
        <ul class="mt-1 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
          <li>DAU / MAU bands and active sessions only.</li>
          <li>Public vs private post volume.</li>
          <li>Reverse-chronological vs algorithmic feed usage (adults).</li>
          <li>Algorithmic opt-in rate; parent-approved minor algorithmic opt-ins.</li>
          <li>Visibility transitions (private→public, public→private).</li>
          <li>Friend connections and public vs secret follows (aggregate).</li>
          <li>EUDI verification completions and failures.</li>
          <li>API / feed error rates; latency and infrastructure capacity.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="m-0 font-medium">Post interaction (anonymised)</p>
        <ul class="mt-1 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
          <li>
            Per-post on the public lane (for the author): views, reactions, comments,
            shares.
          </li>
          <li>
            Optional platform-wide rollups: total views, reactions, comments, shares
            per day.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="m-0">
          Per-post counts only — no author-level engagement scores or interest graphs.
          No public-style statistics on private posts.
        </p>
      </EditorialTodo>

      <H3 id="part-iv-public-vs-private-metrics"
        >Public vs Private Lane Implications</H3
      >
      <p>
        On the public lane, metrics may include coarse content-volume counts. On
        the private lane, no message content or per-recipient engagement graphs are
        collected. Encrypted payload relay volume is not counted — ciphertext is deleted
        from the server after the client receives it and backs it up.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iv-what-is-forbidden">What Is Forbidden</H3>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Per-user engagement graphs and interest inference from aggregate data.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Re-identification of individuals from aggregated statistics.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>Third-party analytics SDKs.</ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Mining private ciphertext metadata beyond delivery health.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <H3 id="part-iv-anonymization-and-transparency"
        >Anonymization and Transparency</H3
      >
      <p>
        Aggregation thresholds prevent fine-grained segmentation that could
        deanonymize small groups. Users who explicitly opt in may contribute
        broader anonymized data to help improve platform models — but only with
        informed, affirmative consent.
      </p>
      <p>
        Aggregate metrics are reported publicly. The platform publishes an
        annual disclosure of its data practices.
      </p>
      </ArticleDetails>

      <DesignPrinciple id="principle-iv" title="Metrics Without Surveillance">
        <p>
          Operational metrics are necessary; individual surveillance is not. The
          platform measures its own health through anonymized counters — never
          through per-user behavioral dossiers or interest graphs derived from
          aggregate data.
        </p>
      </DesignPrinciple>
    </ArticlePart>

    <ArticlePart
      id="part-v"
      part="Part V"
      title="System Synthesis and Open Governance"
    >
      <SectionLead slot="lead">
        Identity, dual lanes, and minimal metrics synthesize into an open-source
        system governed by a non-profit steward. GDPR, eIDAS 2.0, and DSA
        obligations are met by construction—chronological defaults, parental
        approval for minors, verified identity, and auditable recommender
        transparency.
      </SectionLead>

      <ArticleDetails
        headings={[
          { id: 'part-v-component-overview', title: 'Component Overview' },
          {
            id: 'part-v-synthesizing-the-social-argument',
            title: 'Synthesizing the Social Argument',
          },
          {
            id: 'part-v-open-source-and-auditability',
            title: 'Open Source and Auditability',
          },
          { id: 'part-v-compliance', title: 'Compliance Framing' },
          {
            id: 'part-v-compliance-gdpr',
            title: 'General Data Protection Regulation (EU) 2016/679',
          },
          { id: 'part-v-compliance-eidas', title: 'eIDAS 2.0' },
          {
            id: 'part-v-compliance-dsa',
            title: 'Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)',
          },
          { id: 'part-v-honest-limitations', title: 'Honest Limitations' },
        ]}
      >
      <H3 id="part-v-component-overview">Component Overview</H3>
      <p>
        The architecture ties together identity verification, dual interaction
        lanes, and anonymized metrics into a single coherent system. Every layer
        enforces the same principle: serve users' benefit, protect privacy, verify
        humanity.
      </p>

      <DiagramPlaceholder
        title="Full platform architecture synthesis"
        description="Identity layer → public lane → private lane → anonymized aggregate metrics."
      />

      <H3 id="part-v-synthesizing-the-social-argument"
        >Synthesizing the Social Argument</H3
      >
      <p>
        As <InternalLink
          href="/social-paper#part-viii-synthesizing-the-vision"
          paper={sp}
          section={spVIII}
          heading="Synthesizing the Vision"
        /> argues, repairing the digital town square requires addressing systemic
        failures concurrently—not one fix at a time. The architecture below maps
        each argument to a technical implementation:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <InternalLink
            href="/social-paper#part-i-the-new-dna-local-only-primitives"
            paper={sp}
            section={spI}
            heading="The New DNA: Local-Only Primitives"
          /> — on-device intent vectors, probabilistic seen-post filters, E2EE
          cross-device sync, and a private lane encrypted by the <ExternalLink href="https://signal.org/docs/" label="Signal Protocol Documentation" /> (Part III).
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <InternalLink
            href="/social-paper#part-ii-algorithmic-interventions"
            paper={sp}
            section={spII}
            heading="Algorithmic Interventions"
          /> — reverse-chronological default, algorithmic feeds only by explicit
          adult opt-in, local intent with no persistent server-side profiling
          (Part II).
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <InternalLink
            href="/social-paper#part-iii-guardrails-for-minor-accounts"
            paper={sp}
            section={spIII}
            heading="Guardrails for Minor Accounts"
          /> — chronological default for minors, curated allowlists, parental
          validation on every connection, and algorithmic recommendations only with
          parental approval (<ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32022R2065" label="Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)" /> safeguards in <InternalLink
            href="#part-v-compliance-dsa"
            paper="Technical Paper"
            section="Part V: System Synthesis and Open Governance"
            heading="Digital Services Act (DSA)"
          />).
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <InternalLink
            href="/social-paper#part-iv-the-ease-of-creating-and-managing-bots"
            paper={sp}
            section={spIV}
            heading="The Ease of Creating and Managing Bots"
          /> — EUDI Wallet verification, selective disclosure, zero-knowledge
          proofs, and one human, one account (Part I).
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <InternalLink
            href="/social-paper#part-v-pure-capitalism-and-social-capitalism"
            paper={sp}
            section={spV}
            heading="Pure Capitalism and Social Capitalism"
          /> — a non-profit steward with no ad-driven feed ownership; brands
          participate as guests, not architectural dictators.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <InternalLink
            href="/social-paper#part-vi-professional-advice-and-verified-credentials"
            paper={sp}
            section={spVI}
            heading="Professional Advice and Verified Credentials"
          /> — speech governed by applicable law, with EUDI Wallet credentials required
          for directive medical, legal, or regulated financial advice (Part I).
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <InternalLink href="/social-paper#part-vii" paper={sp} section={spVII} /> — open-source code, public audits, and non-profit governance that
          bind obligations by inspectable architecture and institution design—not
          revocable policy promises.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <H3 id="part-v-open-source-and-auditability"
        >Open Source and Auditability</H3
      >
      <p>
        The platform's code, algorithms, and identity flows are open to public
        scrutiny. Independent security audits, a public bug bounty program, and
        transparent governance ensure the architecture described in this paper
        matches the system users actually run. As <InternalLink href="/social-paper#part-vii" paper={sp} section={spVII} /> documents, commercial platforms can reverse privacy promises while
        budgeting penalties as operating cost. Inspectable code and independent
        audits are how users and regulators verify behavior without trusting
        marketing copy.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-v-compliance">Compliance Framing</H3>
      <p>
        The platform is designed in alignment with applicable law. As <InternalLink
          href="/social-paper#part-i-the-scarcity-of-privacy-primitives"
          paper={sp}
          section={spI}
          heading="The Scarcity of Privacy Primitives"
        /> argues, privacy obligations on the open web are imposed legally; the
        architecture below is how compliant systems implement them. Legal
        compliance is necessary but not sufficient: as <InternalLink
          href="/social-paper#part-vii"
          paper={sp}
          section={spVII}
        /> documents, fines and settlements do not bind the next product reversal. As
        described in <InternalLink
          href="/social-paper#part-v-pure-capitalism-and-social-capitalism"
          paper={sp}
          section={spV}
          heading="Pure Capitalism and Social Capitalism"
        />, a non-profit steward, combined with architecture that enforces privacy and
        safety by construction, aims to bind behavior—not by press release, but by
        inspectable design. As <InternalLink
          href="/social-paper#part-vi"
          paper={sp}
          section={spVI}
        /> argues, speech on the platform is governed by applicable law—not by discretionary
        Terms of Service or informal government flagging, with professional advice gated
        by verified licenses as described in <InternalLink
          href="/social-paper#part-vi-professional-advice-and-verified-credentials"
          paper={sp}
          section={spVI}
          heading="Professional Advice and Verified Credentials"
        />. The following frameworks inform its design — not as checkboxes, but as structural
        constraints on how the system is built and operated.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-v-compliance-gdpr">
        <ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32016R0679" label="General Data Protection Regulation (EU) 2016/679" />
      </H3>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Privacy by design and by default</U> (<ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504#art_25" label="GDPR Article 25 — Data Protection by Design and by Default" />) — protective settings are the default; personal data processing is limited
          to what the architecture requires.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Data minimization</U> (<ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504#art_5" label="GDPR Article 5 — Principles Relating to Processing of Personal Data" />) — only data adequate, relevant, and necessary for stated purposes is processed.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Data Protection Impact Assessments</U> (<ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504#art_35" label="GDPR Article 35 — Data Protection Impact Assessment" />) — conducted for high-risk processing activities, including identity verification
          and feed personalization.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <H3 id="part-v-compliance-eidas">eIDAS 2.0</H3>
      <p>
        Digital identity verification follows <ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1183" label="Regulation (EU) 2024/1183 (eIDAS 2.0)" /> (<ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1183" label="Regulation (EU) 2024/1183 (eIDAS 2.0)" />) and the <ExternalLink href="https://commission.europa.eu/topics/digital-economy-and-society/european-digital-identity_en" label="European Digital Identity Wallet — European Commission" />: selective disclosure, zero-knowledge proofs where feasible, and no storage
        of raw credentials beyond what verification requires.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-v-compliance-dsa">
        Digital Services Act (<ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32022R2065" label="Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)" />)
      </H3>
      <p>
        Serving EU users as a very large online platform, it is designed with <ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32022R2065" label="Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)" /> (the <ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32022R2065" label="Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)" />) in mind — particularly its obligations around the protection of minors and
        transparency of recommender systems.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U
            ><ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:32022R2065#art_28" label="DSA Article 28 — Online Interface Design and Organisation" /> — Protection of minors:</U
          > the architecture enforces age verification through the identity layer
          (selective disclosure or zero-knowledge proof of legal age). Accounts verified
          as belonging to a minor receive a chronological feed by default; algorithmic
          recommendations are available only when approved by a parent or guardian.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>No profiling of minors:</U> activity data from minor accounts is used
          solely to deliver core platform functionality and safety enforcement — not
          to infer interests, build profiles, or train recommendation models.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U
            >Recommender system transparency (<ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:32022R2065#art_27" label="DSA Article 27 — Recommender System Transparency" />):</U
          > for adult users who explicitly opt in to algorithmic feeds, the platform
          discloses what data drives recommendations, the purpose and logic of the system,
          and how to deactivate the feature. The feed pipeline described in Part II —
          vector query, seen-post filtering, ranking — is open to public audit.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Additional minor safeguards:</U> curated allowlists gate which public
          accounts minors may interact with; every new connection requires parent
          validation; parents control guardrails within platform minimums; privacy
          settings default to the most protective configuration; direct messaging
          from non-connected users is restricted; and regular technical audits verify
          that algorithmic recommendation pathways for minors require parental approval
          and are not accessible without it through standard interfaces or APIs.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <ExternalLink href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32022R2065" label="Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)" /> compliance is not a layer applied after the fact. Chronological defaults,
        parental approval for minor algorithmic recommendations, and verified identity
        are architectural decisions that satisfy these obligations by construction.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-v-honest-limitations">Honest Limitations</H3>
      <p>
        This paper states principles; the build will test them. Identity
        frameworks outside the European Union will be integrated as they mature.
        The local-first model requires capable client devices. Age verification
        and cross-border identity interoperability remain active areas of work. A
        sustainable funding model for the stewarding non-profit organization
        must be proven in practice. That organization will initially be funded
        by donations and grants—not venture capital chasing a network-effects
        exit—because accountability and ethics cannot depend on shareholders
        demanding capital gains above user benefit.
      </p>
      </ArticleDetails>

      <DesignPrinciple id="principle-v" title="The Commitment to Build" final>
        <p>
          Identifying the necessary architecture is only the first step. We are
          building this platform in public — open source, transparent, and
          governed by a non-profit organization whose sole mission is to steward
          a social network engineered for users' benefit. That structure removes
          the pressure to harvest data, hook attention, or optimize for outrage.
        </p>
        <p>
          The principles in this paper are the blueprint. Open code is the proof
          of accountability—not corporate self-certification.
        </p>
      </DesignPrinciple>
    </ArticlePart>

